cause her heart wasn't working  properly we would  more than likely lead to a fatal heart  attack but she was going to die anyway  so the three of us discussed it and we  and we came to the only conclusion that  there was nothing left to lose we had to  try it  and the doctor date said stay because if  anything goes wrong it will go wrong  really quickly he came back after now  and said she was still fighting still  surviving go home will ring you if  anything bad happens and that night the  phone didn't ring  they turns her again the next day for  about 15 hours and the phone didn't ring  Monday nights and then on Tuesday  evening that the house phone did ring  and it could only be that the hospital I  remember running for the phone my heart  pumping I couldn't I couldn't hear what  was being said it was a lovely nurse I  believe called Abbey who started the  conversation with can I just say what a  remarkable woman your wife is and she  was responding well to being turned that  the oxygen was better now that blood  flow was better she was still on life  support but the nurse said for the first  time they were optimistic not out of the  woods yet and then Adam I think was it  another day or two later the phone rang  again you saw hospital ICU come up on  your phone your heart must have been  pounding again and then you go to a  voice at the other end of the phone well  and said would you would you like to  speak to your to your wife which would  knock me back but they issue as though  just bringing her round from  life-support being unconscious and they  wanted to see how aware she was so I  spoke to her told her that we we loved  her she had to keep fighting and the  nurse said when she heard my voice she  opened her eyes and when I said you have  to keep fighting she mumbled okay but  they that staff there were just  brilliant one one nurse gave me her  personal mobile number so I could sends  her a picture of myself and the kids  which you then printed out taped to some  of the equipment around Stacy's bed and  every hour or so someone would go up to  Stacy and say Adam the kids they're  there with you their fight  you have to keep fighting and Stacy said  she remembered some of those messages  though they were just all incredible and  what's remarkable is given the pressures  that these doctors and nurses are under  as you say the amount of time that they  took to communicate with you keep you  informed all the time and to keep Stacy  in that conversation as well actually I  I don't know how they do it to say thank  you to the whole team there that saved  your wife life wife's life is just  inadequate and the main reason that we  we've done these interviews is to give  back to say thank you and also if people  can donate to go to the Just Giving comm  slash arts charity to which we've cross  hospital is part off to donate anything  anything they can because these these  guys are risking their lives day in day  out and if they're going back to their  friends to live with their friends and  family they're risking their lives as  well it says it's truly heroic so a few  days after you'd spoken to Stacy as she  was beginning to wake up she was  transferred out of ICU  yes about a week she was in ICU for just  over a week then went through the high  dependency units for a day or two and  then went to a respiratory ward and then  about 20 days later I got a call from  Stacy saying would you come and pick me  up which was just incredible I don't I  don't think I'd let myself believe that  there could be a happy ending after  going through that horror of being told  your your wife is going to die and then  having to tell your kids to give  yourself that that hope was I didn't  want it to risk being brought down so  quickly even though I know how strong  she is which is proved she's really good  she's exhausted she's very weak she says  she feels like she's been hit by several  trucks and but everyday I can  see that she's getting slightly stronger  yeah we've got time it whether it takes  six weeks or six months we've got time  for her to recuperate which I'm just  it's only grateful for and I imagine you  will be clapping louder than most this  evening come eight o'clock Adam the  first the first day Stacy was home was  last Thursday so at 8 o'clock we went to  the window and we all clouds absolutely  yeah we'll be putting some extra effort  into it Adam thank you very much indeed  for telling your story an incredibly  traumatic story but one with a happy and  positive ending we wish safe see all the  best with her continued recovery  Roger thank you thank you
 

